@flexiarg operator/resume-by-restating-the-goal
@title Resume The Suspended Thread By Restating Its Goal
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-4PSq9z turn-XDzSjm
@parent process-coherence/status-refresh-before-work
@context A long digression (here the Minard campaign) has occupied the session, and the operator returns to an earlier thread.

! conclusion: Mark the resumption ('before we started on X, what we were talking about was Y') and restate the goal and its purpose ('so that we can make a model of the operator in AIF terms') before the new content.

  + IF:
    The shared context of the suspended thread may have decayed on both sides.

  + HOWEVER:
    Jumping straight to the new sub-problem risks the agent solving against a stale or partial frame; but re-narrating the whole history wastes the turn. The middle move is to restate just the goal-frame — what the thread was FOR — in one sentence.

  + THEN:
    Mark the resumption ('before we started on X, what we were talking about was Y') and restate the goal and its purpose ('so that we can make a model of the operator in AIF terms') before the new content.

  + BECAUSE:
    The parent refreshes mission status against reality before work resumes; the operator's restatement is the conversational form of that refresh — it re-synchronises the shared frame at the resumption seam so the following diagnosis and proposal are evaluated against the right goal.
